# the argument of epictetus-4.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
logic	s5
atom	worry	one says "What shall I do? how will it be? how will it turn out? will this happen?", i.e. there is room for such anxious words
atom	concern_external	one is turning oneself to things which are not within the power of the will
atom	rule	one desires nothing which belongs to others and tries to avoid none of the things which are not in one's power
atom	fail	one fails in one's desires and falls into that which one would avoid
atom	choose_fail	one chooses to desire that which one will fail to obtain and to attempt to avoid that into which one will fall
atom	assent_in_power	not assenting to the false and not turning from the truth is in one's own power
atom	anxious_assent_warranted	there is reason to be anxious about assent and truth
P1	S1	all_difficulty_external	kind empirical
atom	all_difficulty_external	the difficulties and helplessness of all men are about external things
P2	S3	worry → concern_external	kind empirical
P3	S6	assent_in_power	kind empirical
P4	S5	assent_in_power → ¬anxious_assent_warranted
P5	S8	¬choose_fail → rule	kind normative
P6	S9	□(¬rule → fail)
# rule as stated in S8 just is not desiring or avoiding what lies outside the power of the will
A1	-	rule → ¬concern_external	kind conceptual
# the text takes it that no one chooses to fail in desire and fall into what he avoids
A2	-	¬choose_fail
C1	S8	rule	from P5 A2
C	S10	¬worry	from C1 A1 P2
